    "keyNotes": "• Workshop Safety Principles:\n  - Safety is the state of being protected against physical, chemical, or biological hazards in the workshop.\n  - Three categories of safety: Safety to self/personnel, safety to tools and equipment, and safety to the working environment.\n• Causes of Workshop Accidents:\n  - Unsafe acts (human error): Horseplay, carelessness, lack of concentration, failure to wear PPE, using broken or incorrect tools, fatigue.\n  - Unsafe conditions (environmental): Slippery or wet floors, poor lighting (inadequate illumination), obstructed gangways, lack of ventilation, ungrounded electrical wiring, unguarded machine parts.\n• Personal Protective Equipment (PPE):\n  - Eye & Face Protection: Safety goggles, face shields (protects against flying chips, sparks, chemical splashes).\n  - Hearing Protection: Earplugs, earmuffs (protects against decibel levels > 85 dB).\n  - Body & Hand Protection: Leather apron, overall/overcoat (buttoned up, no loose ties or sleeves), heavy-duty leather gloves for welding and sheet metal, rubber gloves for chemicals.\n  - Foot Protection: Steel-toed safety boots with slip-resistant soles (protects against dropped heavy tools and sharp offcuts).\n  - Respiratory Protection: Dust masks, respirator cartridges (protects against wood dust, paint vapor, and toxic fumes).\n• Fire Safety & Classes of Fires:\n  - Class A: Ordinary combustibles (wood, paper, cloth, plastics) -> Extinguished using Water, Foam, or ABC Dry Powder.\n  - Class B: Flammable liquids (petrol, kerosene, paints, solvents, thinners) -> Extinguished using Foam, CO2, Dry Powder. (NEVER USE WATER).\n  - Class C: Flammable gases (butane, propane, acetylene) -> Extinguished by shutting supply valve, CO2, Dry Powder.\n  - Class D: Combustible metals (magnesium, potassium, sodium) -> Extinguished using Special Dry Powder extinguishers.\n  - Electrical Fires: Fires involving energized electrical circuits -> Extinguished using Carbon Dioxide (CO2) or ABC Dry Powder.\n• First Aid in the Workshop:\n  - Definition: Immediate treatment given to an injured person before professional medical aid arrives.\n  - Contents of First Aid Box: Sterile dressings, bandages, adhesive plasters, antiseptic solution (Savlon, methylated spirit), burn cream, scissors, tweezers, cotton wool, disposable gloves.\n  - ABC of resuscitation: Airway, Breathing, Circulation.\n  - Immediate action for severe bleeding: Apply direct pressure with sterile dressing, elevate the injured limb above heart level, seek medical help.",
