    "keyNotes": "• Ferrous Metals:\n  - Definition: Metals containing iron (Fe) as their chief constituent; generally magnetic and prone to corrosion (rust) when exposed to oxygen and moisture.\n  - Pig Iron: Crude raw product extracted from iron ore in a blast furnace; high carbon content (3.5% - 4.5%), brittle.\n  - Cast Iron: Carbon content 2% - 4%; hard, brittle, cannot be bent or forged, excellent compressive strength, self-lubricating graphite flakes. Uses: Engine blocks, machine beds, vices, manhole covers.\n  - Wrought Iron: Purest commercial iron (< 0.1% carbon); malleable, ductile, tough, rust-resistant. Uses: Ornamental gates, chains, crane hooks.\n  - Carbon Steels:\n    * Low Carbon / Mild Steel (0.15% - 0.3% C): Ductile, malleable, easily welded. Uses: Roofing sheets, girders, nails, bolts, car bodies.\n    * Medium Carbon Steel (0.3% - 0.6% C): Tougher, harder. Uses: Axles, rails, crankshafts, hammer heads.\n    * High Carbon Steel / Tool Steel (0.6% - 1.5% C): Extremely hard, holds cutting edge. Uses: Chisels, hacksaw blades, drills, files.\n• Non-Ferrous Metals:\n  - Definition: Metals containing no iron; non-magnetic, corrosion-resistant, lightweight, excellent electrical and thermal conductivity.\n  - Copper: Reddish brown, highly ductile, second best electrical conductor. Uses: Electrical wires, domestic water pipes, printed circuit boards.\n  - Aluminium: Silvery white, low density (approx 2.7 g/cm³), self-passivating protective oxide film, excellent thermal conductor. Uses: Window frames, cooking pots, aircraft fuselage, overhead power transmission lines.\n  - Lead: Bluish grey, heavy, soft, low melting point (327°C), radiation barrier. Uses: Car battery plates, roof flashing, radiation shields.\n  - Zinc: Bluish white, corrosion-resistant coating for steel (galvanization).\n  - Tin: Soft, malleable, silvery; used in tin-plating food cans.\n• Alloys:\n  - Definition: Homogeneous mixture of two or more metals, or a metal with a non-metal, fused to enhance mechanical properties.\n  - Brass: Copper (approx 65-70%) + Zinc (30-35%). Harder than copper, golden luster, corrosion-resistant. Uses: Door handles, musical instruments, plumbing valves.\n  - Bronze: Copper (approx 88-90%) + Tin (10-12%). Tough, wear-resistant. Uses: Statues, ship propellers, bearings, bells.\n  - Duralumin: Aluminium (94%) + Copper (4%) + Magnesium (1%) + Manganese (1%). Lightweight with steel-like tensile strength. Uses: Aircraft frames.\n  - Solder: Tin (60%) + Lead (40%) [or lead-free tin-copper-silver]. Low melting point alloy for joining electrical wiring and copper pipes.\n• Plastics (Polymers):\n  - Thermoplastics: Linear polymer chains held by weak intermolecular van der Waals forces; soften upon heating and solidify upon cooling repeatedly without chemical decomposition; recyclable.\n    * Examples: Polyethylene (Polythene - bags, bottles), Polyvinyl Chloride (PVC - conduit pipes, electrical insulation), Polystyrene (disposable cups, packaging foam), Acrylic (Perspex - signage, goggles).\n  - Thermosetting Plastics: Cross-linked covalent polymer molecular network formed during heating/curing; once set by heat and pressure, they cannot be remelted or reshaped without burning/charring; non-recyclable.\n    * Examples: Bakelite (Phenol formaldehyde - electrical plugs, sockets, saucepan handles), Melamine (scratch-resistant kitchen plates, laminates), Epoxy resin (high-strength adhesives, fiberglass matrix), Urea-formaldehyde (electrical switches, wood glue).",
