    "keyNotes": "• Technical Drawing Instruments & Board Preparation:\n  - Drawing Board: Flat softwood surface with an accurate straight ebony edge on the left side for guiding the T-square head.\n  - T-Square: Comprises stock (head) and blade; stock must always be pressed firmly against the left working edge of the board. Used for drawing horizontal lines and supporting set squares.\n  - Set Squares: \n    * 30°/60° Set Square: Contains angles 30°, 60°, and 90°.\n    * 45° Set Square: Contains two 45° angles and one 90° angle.\n    * Combined usage enables drawing angles in increments of 15° (15°, 75°, 105°, 120°, 135°, 150°, 165°).\n  - Compasses & Dividers: Large bow compass for drawing circles and arcs; dividers for transferring dimensions and dividing lines into equal segments.\n  - Pencils: \n    * Hard pencils (2H, 3H, 4H): Light construction lines, projection lines, dimension lines, center lines.\n    * Medium pencils (H, HB): Visible outlines, borders, lettering, arrowheads.\n    * Soft pencils (B, 2B): Freehand sketching, artistic shading (avoided in engineering drafting).\n• Standard Line Types (ISO / BS 8888):\n  - Continuous Thick Line (0.5 - 0.7 mm): Visible outlines and visible edges.\n  - Continuous Thin Line (0.25 - 0.35 mm): Construction lines, projection lines, dimension lines, extension lines, hatching/cross-section lines, leader lines.\n  - Dashed Thin Line: Hidden outlines and hidden edges (equal dashes 3 mm long with 1 mm spaces).\n  - Long Dash-Dot Thin Line (Chain Line): Center lines, axes of symmetry, pitch circles (long dash 10-12 mm, gap 1 mm, dot/short dash 1 mm, gap 1 mm).\n  - Continuous Thin Irregular / Wavy Line: Short break lines.\n  - Thin Line with Zigzags: Long break lines.\n• Title Block and Lettering:\n  - Position: Lower right corner or along the bottom border of the drawing sheet.\n  - Information required: Name of student, school, date, drawing title, scale (e.g. 1:1, 1:2, 2:1), projection symbol (1st or 3rd angle), sheet number.\n  - Standard lettering: Single-stroke Gothic uppercase letters, uniform height (typically 5 mm or 7 mm for titles, 3.5 mm for notes/dimensions), upright or 75° inclined.\n• Freehand Sketching Techniques:\n  - Oblique Sketching: Front face drawn true shape; receding axis drawn at 45° (or 30°); Cavalier projection (full scale on receding axis) vs Cabinet projection (half scale on receding axis to reduce distortion).\n  - Isometric Sketching: Based on isometric axes where three principal axes meet at 120° (two horizontal axes inclined at 30° to horizontal line, one vertical axis). All parallel lines remain parallel.",
