    "keyNotes": "• Geometrical Bisecting:\n  - Perpendicular Bisector of a Straight Line AB: With compass center at A and radius > half AB, draw arcs above and below AB. With same radius and center at B, draw intersecting arcs. Join intersection points with a straight line.\n  - Bisecting Any Angle ∠ABC: With center B, draw an arc intersecting BA at P and BC at Q. With centers P and Q and equal radii > half PQ, draw intersecting arcs at R. Join BR. Ray BR bisects ∠ABC.\n• Construction of Standard Angles (Ruler and Compass Only):\n  - 60° Angle: Draw baseline AB. With center A, draw arc cutting AB at C. With same radius and center C, cut arc at D. Ray AD forms 60° with AB.\n  - 30° Angle: Bisect a 60° angle.\n  - 90° Angle: Construct perpendicular bisector or erect perpendicular at a point using intersecting arcs from points equidistant on baseline.\n  - 45° Angle: Bisect a 90° angle.\n  - 75° Angle: Bisect the 30° interval between 60° and 90° (60° + 15° = 75°).\n  - 105° Angle: Bisect the 30° interval between 90° and 120° (90° + 15° = 105°).\n  - 120° Angle: Step off two consecutive 60° arcs along a semi-circle.\n• Construction of Triangles:\n  - SSS (Three sides given: a, b, c): Draw base AB = c. With center A, strike arc radius b; with center B, strike arc radius a. Intersection gives vertex C.\n  - SAS (Two sides and included angle): Draw base AB = c. Construct angle θ at A. Mark point C such that AC = b. Join BC.\n  - ASA (One side and two adjacent angles): Draw base AB = c. Construct angle α at A and angle β at B. The rays intersect at vertex C.\n• Quadrilaterals & Regular Polygons:\n  - Quadrilaterals: Four-sided plane figures whose interior angles sum to 360° (Square, Rectangle, Parallelogram, Rhombus, Trapezium, Kite).\n  - Construction of an Equilateral Triangle on base AB: Arcs of radius AB from A and B meet at C.\n  - Construction of a Regular Hexagon in a circle of radius R: The side length of a regular hexagon equals the radius R of its circumscribing circle. Step off radius R six times around circumference.\n  - General method for Regular Polygons (e.g. Pentagon, Heptagon): Divide diameter AB of circumscribing circle into N equal parts. With centers A and B and radius AB, draw intersecting arcs at P. Draw line from P through division 2 to cut the circumference at C. Line AC is one side of the N-sided polygon.",
