    "orderIndex": 7,
    "title": "Cutting, Impelling and Shaping Tools (Saws, Chisels, Files & Planes)",
    "description": "Explore operating mechanics, maintenance, and safe manipulation of hand saws, bench planes, wood and cold chisels, files, rasps, hammers, and screwdrivers.",
    "isFreeTrial": true,
    "isVip": false,
    "youtubeUrl": "https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=0bT4r_aZt-g",
    "youtubeId": "0bT4r_aZt-g",
    "keyNotes": "• Saws & Sawing Geometry:\n  - Kerf: The width of the slot cut by saw teeth; teeth are alternate 'set' (bent left and right) so the cut is wider than the blade thickness, preventing the blade from binding/jamming.\n  - Woodworking Saws:\n    * Rip Saw: Large teeth (4-6 TPI - teeth per inch) shaped like chisels; cuts along the grain.\n    * Cross-cut Saw: Knife-like triangular teeth (6-8 TPI); cuts across the grain fibers cleanly.\n    * Tenon / Back Saw: Stiff brass or steel spine along top; fine teeth (12-14 TPI); cuts joints, tenons, and shoulders.\n    * Dovetail Saw: Smaller, thinner tenon saw (15-20 TPI); precision dovetail joint cutting.\n    * Coping Saw & Bow Saw: Thin flexible blade held in tension in a C-shaped steel frame; cuts tight internal curves and shapes.\n  - Metalworking Saws:\n    * Hacksaw: Rigid or adjustable tubular frame; high-speed steel blade with 14, 18, 24, or 32 TPI. Teeth must point forwards (cuts on forward push stroke). Rule of thumb: at least 3 teeth must engage the metal thickness simultaneously.\n• Planes & Planing (Woodwork):\n  - Bench Planes: Jack Plane (approx 350 mm long - rapid stock removal, truing rough wood); Fore Plane / Trying Plane (450-600 mm - truing long edges); Smoothing Plane (200-250 mm - final finishing before sandpapering).\n  - Special Planes: Block plane (low angle blade, planes end grain); Rebate / Rabbet plane (cuts stepped recesses along edges); Plough plane (cuts grooves parallel to edge).\n  - Blade Angles: Grinding angle (bevel) = 25°; Honing angle = 30°; Frog seating angle = 45°. Cap iron (chipbreaker) curled tip set 1 mm from cutting edge to curl and break shavings.\n• Chisels:\n  - Wood Chisels: Firmer chisel (flat rectangular cross-section for general heavy chiselling); Bevel-edge chisel (bevelled edges allow entry into acute corners of dovetail sockets); Mortise chisel (thick, robust cross-section to withstand heavy mallet blows when chopping mortises).\n  - Cold Chisels (Metalwork): Hardened and tempered tool steel. Flat chisel (cutting sheet metal, chipping welds); Cross-cut / Cape chisel (cutting keyways and narrow grooves); Diamond point chisel (cutting V-grooves and cleaning inside corners); Half-round nose chisel (cutting oil channels in bearings).\n• Files & Rasps (Metal & Wood):\n  - File Cuts: Single-cut (smooth finishing on hard metals); Double-cut (rapid material removal on steel); Rasp-cut (triangular raised individual teeth for wood and leather).\n  - File Grades: Rough, Bastard, Second-cut, Smooth, Dead Smooth.\n  - Cross filing (material removal across workpiece) vs Draw filing (sliding file sideways along workpiece with both hands for satin finish).\n  - Safety: NEVER use a file without a securely fitted handle (tang can penetrate the palm). Clean clogged teeth with a file card.\n• Impelling Tools:\n  - Hammers: Claw hammer (driving and extracting nails in woodwork); Warrington hammer (cross-pein drives small panel pins into tight rebates); Ball-pein hammer (flat face strikes punches, ball pein rivets metal); Mallet (hardwood or rubber head, strikes wood chisel handles without splitting them).\n  - Screwdrivers: Slotted / Flathead, Phillips (cross-head), Pozidriv, Torx (star). Match driver size to screw slot precisely.",
    "examples": [
      {
        "id": "ex-jhs2ct-t7-1",
        "title": "Selecting Hacksaw Blades for Metalwork",
        "problem": "A student needs to cut (a) a solid 30 mm mild steel bar, and (b) a 1.2 mm thick hollow steel pipe. Choose the appropriate hacksaw blade TPI for each and explain why.",
        "stepByStepSolution": [
          "Step 1: Apply the 3-tooth rule: At least 3 consecutive teeth must be in contact with the material cross-section at all times to prevent teeth from straddling and stripping off.",
          "Step 2: For solid 30 mm bar: Large contact area; choose a coarse blade with 14 or 18 TPI. Coarse teeth provide large gullets to clear metal chips without clogging.",
          "Step 3: For 1.2 mm thin wall pipe: Small contact thickness; choose a fine blade with 32 TPI (32 teeth per 25.4 mm = approx 0.8 mm pitch). This ensures at least 2 to 3 teeth rest on the 1.2 mm wall.",
          "Step 4: Conclusion: Use 18 TPI for the solid bar and 32 TPI for the thin hollow pipe."
        ],
        "keyTakeaway": "Use coarse TPI (14-18) for solid thick stock and fine TPI (24-32) for thin sheet and tubing."
      },
      {
        "id": "ex-jhs2ct-t7-2",
        "title": "Adjusting and Using a Jack Plane",
        "problem": "When planing a timber board, the plane skips and gouges the surface, tearing wood fibers. Identify two possible causes and outline correct adjustment steps.",
        "stepByStepSolution": [
          "Step 1: Check blade sharpness: A dull iron tears fibers rather than shaving them cleanly. Hone cutting bevel on oilstone to 30° until a sharp burr is removed.",
          "Step 2: Adjust blade projection: Use the brass adjusting nut to withdraw the blade until the cutting edge projects barely a hair's breadth beyond the sole.",
          "Step 3: Adjust lateral alignment: Move the lateral adjustment lever until the blade edge is parallel with the sole mouth.",
          "Step 4: Check grain direction: Always plane in the downhill direction of the wood grain; planing against the grain forces the blade under fibers, causing tear-out."
        ],
        "keyTakeaway": "Plane with the grain with a finely projected, razor-sharp iron to produce continuous paper-thin shavings."
      }
    ]
