    "keyNotes": "• Classification of Sewing Tools and Equipment:\n  - Measuring Tools:\n    * Flexible Tape Measure: 150 cm (60 inches) fiberglass or vinyl coated tape with metal ends; takes body measurements.\n    * Hem Gauge: Small metal or card ruler with sliding pointer; measures hems, pleats, and buttonholes.\n  - Marking Tools:\n    * Tailor's Chalk: Colored wax or compressed chalk; marks dart lines, seam allowances, and alteration notches.\n    * Tracing Wheel & Dressmaker's Carbon Paper: Toothed wheel transfers pattern lines onto fabric backing.\n  - Cutting Tools:\n    * Dressmaker's Shears: 20-25 cm long blades with one angled handle loop; allows blade to rest flat on table while cutting fabric without lifting it.\n    * Pinking Shears: Zigzag serrated blades; cuts non-fraying seam finishes on woven fabrics.\n    * Embroidery Scissors: Small, pointed 10 cm scissors; snips threads and clips buttonholes.\n    * Seam Ripper: Hooked pointed tool with small protective ball; unpicks mistake stitches safely.\n  - Sewing & Pressing Tools:\n    * Needles (Sharps, Betweens, Crewel): Graded by number (size 7 to 9 for general sewing; lower numbers are thicker).\n    * Thimble: Pitted metal or plastic shield worn on middle finger of sewing hand; pushes needle through thick fabrics without piercing finger.\n    * Pins and Pin Cushion: Steel dressmaker pins hold fabric layers temporarily; emery bag sharpens and removes rust from pins.\n    * Steam Iron & Ironing Board: Sets seams flat, presses out wrinkles during assembly.\n• The Sewing Machine:\n  - Major Parts: Balance wheel (handwheel), spool pin, bobbin winder, thread tension regulator, thread take-up lever, needle clamp, presser foot, feed dog (toothed mechanism that advances fabric), throat plate, bobbin case and shuttle.\n  - Common Faults & Causes:\n    * Needle breaking: Needle inserted backwards, bent needle, wrong needle size for fabric, pulling fabric forcefully while sewing.\n    * Thread snapping: Thread tension too tight, poor quality thread, machine threaded incorrectly, needle eye burred.\n    * Skipped stitches: Needle blunt or inserted upside down, needle too fine for thick thread.\n    * Looped stitches on underside: Upper thread tension too loose or upper thread not routed through tension discs.\n• Classification of Hand Stitches:\n  - Temporary Stitches (Removed after permanent stitching):\n    * Even Basting / Tacking: Equal stitches and spaces (approx 6 mm); holds seams securely for fitting.\n    * Uneven Basting: Long stitch (12 mm) on top, short stitch (3 mm) below; marks center lines.\n    * Diagonal / Tailor's Tacking: Holds slippery layers, coat lapels, or pleats.\n  - Permanent Stitches (Remain permanently in garment):\n    * Running Stitch: Fine, equal continuous stitches (2 mm); used for gathering, easing, and delicate mending.\n    * Backstitch: Strongest hand stitch; resembles machine stitching on top; each stitch overlaps the previous.\n    * Overcasting Stitch: Diagonal loops over raw fabric edges to prevent fraying.\n    * Hemming Stitch: Slanted discreet stitches catching one thread of garment and folded hem edge.\n    * Blind Hemming / Slip Stitch: Invisible stitches hidden inside the fold of a hem.\n  - Decorative Stitches:\n    * Chain stitch, satin stitch, feather stitch, French knots, herringbone stitch, blanket stitch.",
    "examples": [
      {
        "id": "ex-jhs2ct-t11-1",
        "title": "Diagnosing Sewing Machine Thread Looping",
        "problem": "A student finds that while sewing a cotton apron on the machine, loose looped thread nests are forming underneath the fabric seam. Explain the cause and give the step-by-step remedy.",
        "stepByStepSolution": [
          "Step 1: Identify root cause: Counter-intuitively, loops forming on the UNDERSIDE of the fabric are caused by a lack of tension in the UPPER thread, not the bobbin.",
          "Step 2: Lift presser foot: Raise the presser foot lever to release the tension discs completely.",
          "Step 3: Re-thread upper path: Remove upper thread and re-thread systematically: spool pin -> thread guides -> between tension discs -> thread take-up lever -> lower guides -> needle eye.",
          "Step 4: Adjust tension: Turn the upper tension dial clockwise to increase tension slightly.",
          "Step 5: Test sew: Sew on a double layer of scrap fabric until top and bottom stitches lock neatly in the center of the fabric layers."
        ],
        "keyTakeaway": "Bottom thread loops indicate loose upper thread tension or improper upper threading."
      },
      {
        "id": "ex-jhs2ct-t11-2",
        "title": "Executing a Strong Backstitch by Hand",
        "problem": "A student needs to repair a burst sleeve seam without a sewing machine. Describe how to work a backstitch to achieve machine-strength durability.",
        "stepByStepSolution": [
          "Step 1: Secure thread: Knot thread or take two small running stitches at the starting edge.",
          "Step 2: First stitch: Insert needle 3 mm ahead along the seam line and bring it out 3 mm further forward (total 6 mm step).",
          "Step 3: Step backward: Take needle back and insert it into the end of the previous stitch (3 mm back).",
          "Step 4: Bring forward: Pass needle underneath and bring it out 3 mm ahead of the current thread position.",
          "Step 5: Repeat rhythm: Continue the 'back one step, forward two steps' rhythm to form a continuous, unbroken line of stitches on the right side."
        ],
        "keyTakeaway": "Backstitch is the strongest permanent hand stitch because each stitch locks into the previous entry hole."
      }
    ]
